{{smaller|'''Asclepiadean strophe''', a strophe or stanza composed of Asclepiadics with or without other verses, such as Glyconics and Pherecratics.}}

'''Asclepiadic''' (as-klē-pi-ad′ik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ''asclepiad'' + ''-ic.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Of or pertaining to the Asclepiad or Asclepiadic, a kind of verse.

'''II.''' ''n.'' In ''anc. pros.'', a verse consisting of a spondee, two (or three) choriambi, and an iambus; or, according to other authorities, a logaœdic verse consisting of a basis, three cyclic dactyls, of which the second is syncopated (or five cyclic dactyls, of which the second and fourth are syncopated), and a trochaic dipody catalectic. The shorter form is called the ''lesser'', the longer the ''greater'', ''Asclepiadic.''
{{smaller block|<poem>Mæcē- | nās ătăvīs | ēdĭtĕ rē- | gĭbŭs.
Mæcē- | nās ătă- | vīs | ēdĭtĕ | rēgĭbŭs.

Tū nē | quæsīĕrĭs | scīrĕ nĕfās | quēm mĭhĭ quēm | tĭbī.
Tū nē | quæsīĕ | rĭs | scīrĕ nĕ | fās | quēm mĭhĭ | quēm tĭbī.</poem>}}

'''Asclepiads''' (as-klē′pi-adz), ''n. pl.'' [< Gr. Ἀσκληπιάδαι, pl. of Ἀσκληπιάδης, a descendant of Asclepius.] An order of Greek physicians, priests of Asclepius or Æsculapius, the god of medicine, whose descendants they claimed to be. {{smaller|They practised medicine under the reputed inspiration of that deity, and were bound by oath not to reveal the secrets of their art. Also ''Asclepiadæ''.}}
{{smaller block|From these primitive clinical records, the half-priestly, half-philosophic caste of the ''Asclepiads'' compiled the data upon which the earliest generalisations of medicine, as an inductive science, were based.
{{right|''Huxley'', Biol. Sci. and Med.}}}}
